* test: the arm64 pinned CUDA runtime download opts out of the fence

test_live_pinned_cuda_download_pause_resume_and_execute downloads the real
pinned llama.cpp engine and the locked Python toolchain on purpose (only
the Windows arm64 integration lane runs it), so it is an allow_network
test like the other PM downloads. Under the fence the toolchain fetch
from hermes-assets / python-build-standalone was refused.

* test: the upgrade mirror tests' prefetch opts out of the fence

test_mirrors seeds its local PyPI and npm mirrors with S.prefetch, an
in-process download of the pinned wheels / npm tarball from the public
registries (the update under test then reaches only the mirror). The
fence refused files.pythonhosted.org and registry.npmjs.org; mark both
tests allow_network like the other deliberate PM downloads.

* fix(desktop): keep notarizing through dropped connections to Apple

The 2026-11-03 canary (desktop run 41591721476) signed both macOS
builds, and Apple accepted the arm64 submission, but both builds failed
in this hook on network errors it does not retry:

- arm64: `stapler staple` exited 68 with NSURLErrorDomain Code=-1005
  "The network connection was lost." from api.apple-cloudkit.com. Only
  the exit-65 missing-ticket case was retried.
- x64: `notarytool wait` timed out (Code=-1001) at 07:22, 07:24, 07:27
  and 07:38 UTC. Resuming stopped after three pauses (15, 30 and 60 s),
  although the notarization budget is four hours.

Treat NSURLError timeouts, lost connections and unreachable hosts
(-1001, -1003, -1004, -1005, -1006, -1009) as lost contact in both
loops. Keep resuming the same submission, and retrying the staple, with
pauses capped at 120 s until the next pause would cross the existing
four-hour budget. A ticket that has not propagated yet keeps its own
five retries. Verdicts, authentication failures and other stapler
errors still fail at once.

* chore(desktop): list the Web Access progress copy in the desktop i18n key file

The pane's new strings (settings.gateway.webAccess.startElapsed and startStages.*) were missing from the generated locales/_keys.desktop.json, which scripts/i18n-keys.test.mjs checks against the English catalog. Regenerated with npm run i18n:keys.

* docs(ci): document every workflow's triggers, skips and release paths

* fix(desktop): staple over TCP on GitHub's macOS runners

stapler fetches the notarization ticket from api.apple-cloudkit.com
over HTTP/3, and on GitHub's hosted macOS runners those QUIC
connections drop. The staple exits 68 with NSURLErrorDomain -1005
about 7 s in (actions/runner-images#14924, reproduced by GitHub).

The 2026-11-03 arm64 canary failed this way nine seconds after Apple
had created the ticket record. In this workflow's history the error hit
0 of 119 accepted builds before 2026-10-19 and 8 of 28 since, on both
arches. It also failed the verify step's `stapler validate` once.

Before Build and package, load a rule that rejects outbound UDP 443
into a com.apple sub-anchor. The runner's default ruleset already
evaluates com.apple/*, so it stays as it is. stapler then falls back to
TCP for the notarize hook and for the verify step.

Probe run 41655949959 used three macos-15 and three macos-15-intel
machines. Each stapled a copy of Firefox.app 21 times without the rule,
then 20 times with it.

- Without the rule, 60/63 staples worked on arm64 and 32/63 on Intel.
  Every failure was Error 68 with -1005, after about 7 s.
- With the rule, 120/120 worked.
- Every machine sent UDP 443 to Apple's 17.x addresses, and the rule
  rejected 60-85 packets per job.
- The main ruleset was unchanged on all six machines.

* fix(approval): credential-upload rule only counts curl/wget upload flags

The rule matched -d/-F/-T anywhere in the command, so awk -F "$API_KEY"
and cut -d "$SECRET_SEP" were flagged as credential uploads and hard-blocked
in cron, -q and unattended deny modes. A match now counts only when a curl
or wget word precedes the flag inside the same simple command, bounded with
the quote-aware shell parser approvals.deny uses. Wrappers (env, sudo),
programs that run curl (xargs, ssh, watch) and bash -c payloads stay
flagged; rule order and the approval key are unchanged.

* fix(approval): keep flagging uploads when the command word is an expansion

The ownership check added in this branch only counted an upload flag when a
literal curl/wget word preceded it, so $CURL -d "k=$API_KEY", ${CURL} -F,
c=curl; $c -F, "$(which curl)" -T and `which curl` -F stopped being flagged
(they were flagged on main). A word that starts with a parameter expansion,
command substitution or backtick may run curl, so it now counts as an owner.
Literal delimiter commands (awk -F, cut -d, sort -t) stay unflagged.

Reported in review by @Enough1122.

* fix(approval): keep flagging an upload flag inside a re-parsed quoted word

The owner check only looked for a curl/wget word before the flag, so
`eval "curl -d @.env …"`, `ssh host "curl …"`, `su -c "curl …"`,
`sh -c "curl …"` and `script -qc "curl …"` were flagged on main but not on
this branch: the flag sits inside a quoted word another program runs as a
command. A flag inside a word now counts as possibly curl-owned.

* Fixed Windows curl/wget names, mid-word expansions and one parse per command in the upload-flag owner check

* refactor(approval): build upload-flag owner ranges once and bisect them

Same behaviour as the previous commit (its tests pass unchanged), in fewer
lines: each simple command contributes one range from its first word that
may run curl/wget to its end, plus the inside of quoted words; the ranges
are merged once per detection variant and each flag is a bisect lookup.
The per-flag loop over every simple command made a 128 KB line of chained
`cut -d` commands take ~4.3 s; this takes ~2.2 s and grows linearly.

* fix(approval): keep flagging a command name like c${s:=url}

The previous commit treated any word containing "=" as an assignment, so
`c${suffix:=url} -d @.env …` (which runs curl when suffix is unset) was no
longer flagged. Use the existing NAME=value assignment pattern instead, as
the earlier version of this check did.

* fix(approval): look past redirections and globs when finding an upload's owner

Two cases main flagged that this branch had stopped flagging:
- a redirection before curl (`ssh host >/dev/null curl -d @.env …`,
  `eval >/dev/null "curl …"`) ended the owner scan, since the redirection
  reads as an empty word. Skip it the way _iter_shell_command_word_spans does.
- a glob in the command name (`/usr/bin/cur[l]`, `wge*`) may match curl/wget.

