* fix(discord): probe for libopus once, off the event loop

connect() called ctypes.util.find_library("opus") on the gateway loop
whenever opus was not loaded. On Linux find_library runs ldconfig, gcc
and ld subprocesses, and on a host without libopus (the Hermes Cloud
image) it never succeeds, so every Discord connect and reconnect
repeated that blocking work on the loop. On a memory-starved 2 GB
instance both loop-liveness watchdog dumps that restarted the gateway
had the loop thread inside this probe.

Move the opus helpers into adapter_opus.py and probe at most once per
process under a lock; connect() runs it with asyncio.to_thread. A libopus
installed later needs a restart, and the "Opus codec not found" warning
now logs once instead of on every reconnect.

The per-connect probe came in with 0ff1b4ade2 (#1299).

* test(discord): give the opus off-loop test 2 s+ wait bounds

tests/AGENTS.md requires wall-clock bounds of at least 2 s so a busy
runner cannot fail correct code. Also assert the probe actually started
before releasing it.

* fix(desktop): MCP health toast reads backend state and stops nagging about local servers

The background MCP health checker probed every enabled HTTP server every
30 minutes with its own fresh connect. For a server hosted by a local app
(Unreal Editor's built-in MCP server answers only while the editor runs)
that meant a daily "failed its health check" toast whenever the app was
closed, and the probe itself raced startup connects into false failures
(#133155), evicted single-session hosts' live session (#89576) and could
open an OAuth consent tab unattended (#142406).

The sweep now reads mcp.servers.status, the backend's cached runtime view,
and never connects. The status row gains a needs_auth boolean (parked on a
rejected OAuth credential) so the sign-in nudge survives without putting
error text on the wire. Local servers (stdio, or a loopback URL) are never
toasted, extending the existing stdio exemption: the backend already
re-probes a parked server every 5 minutes, so tools return once the app is
open. Remote failures and needs-auth still toast as before.

Fixes #133155, fixes #142406
Refs #89576 (the background-sweep half; the MCP page probe is #89581)

* fix(mcp): status reports a connected-then-parked server as failed

A server that connected, dropped, and parked after exhausting reconnects
records no connect error, so get_mcp_status reported it as "configured" and
the Desktop health sweep (now a read of this status) went quiet for a real
remote outage. A non-live server with a park reason is "failed"; never-tried
servers stay "configured". Found by review on #148395.

* fix(desktop): MCP health sweep reads config and status from one source

The sweep read config through the active registry connection but routed
mcp.servers.status by profile name alone, so with a remote gateway active
and a local profile of the same name, status came from the wrong backend:
a healthy local server could hide a remote outage, or a failing one raise a
false warning. Capture (connection, profile) once per sweep, send both reads
there (requestGatewayForAgent), key transition memory and snoozes by that
source (the local pool keeps the bare profile key), and re-arm when the
active connection changes. Found by review on #148395.

* fix(dashboard): say when Team Gateway relay turned a channel off

* fix(cloud): Telegram on Team Gateway is set up by linking an account to the shared bot

On a Hermes Cloud instance with Team Gateway, every direct messaging adapter is
switched off at gateway start (config_env._relay) and the connector's shared bot
is the only way Telegram, Discord and Slack reach the agent. The Channels page
and the Desktop messaging page still offered "Create with QR" and bot-token
setup, which make a bot the relay never runs: users finished setup, restarted,
and the bot never answered (#141546, #138101).

The instance now mints the connector's one-time `/link <code>` itself:

- gateway/relay/link.py calls /manage/link, /manage/link/state,
  /manage/link-status and /manage/unlink with the per-gateway bearer, and
  builds the shared bot's DM link from GATEWAY_RELAY_BOT_IDS.
- The per-gateway secret lives only in the gateway process, so the gateway
  answers a new `relay-link` control-socket verb and the dashboard routes
  (/api/messaging/relay*) go through it.
- /api/messaging/platforms reports relay_fronted / relay_exclusive per platform.
- Dashboard Channels and Desktop Messaging show a Team Gateway card (link
  button, QR of the bot, the exact message to send, linked accounts with
  Unlink) and hide the direct setup that cannot work under an exclusive relay.

* fix(dashboard): plain Unlink button; the broken-chain icon read as garbled text

* fix(relay-link): review fixes: per-platform panel state, re-link reads as linked, bounded poll, named connector refusals

- Desktop: key the link panel by platform so a pending code never follows the
  user to another platform's page.
- A code that goes inactive before it expires was redeemed: a re-link from an
  already-linked account no longer reads as "expired".
- Polling stops after 5 consecutive errors or at expiry and shows the error.
- Connector 401/403/409 map to named, plain-language errors; 5xx/408/429 are
  retryable (503); unlink answers {unlinked: true}; a silent gateway is worded
  for stopped, too-old and timed-out alike.

* fix(relay-link): the linked toast fires; reload accounts before closing the code

Closing the code (setLink(null)) re-ran the poll effect and set its cancelled
flag before the account reload returned, so a successful link never showed the
confirmation.

* fix(desktop): Team Gateway card shows the backend's refusal sentence, not the IPC envelope

A refused link (e.g. 409 platform_not_fronted, 503 gateway_not_running) reached the card as
"Error invoking remote method 'hermes:api': Error: 409: {"detail":{...}}". Unwrap the IPC
prefix and the router's {detail:{message}} body before showing it.

* fix(desktop): poll errors in the link card use the same readable sentence

* fix(desktop): Team Gateway-managed platforms and profile-scoped link calls

- A platform the relay switches off but the shared bot does not carry (Slack,
  Matrix outside GATEWAY_RELAY_PLATFORMS) now reads 'Managed by Team Gateway'
  with the toggle disabled and the opt-out explained, matching the dashboard
  (#141175); before, its Enable toggle saved and reverted at every restart.
- The link calls carry the settings profile so a profile pointed at another
  host links against that host.

* fix(relay-link): Portal-managed agents link from the Portal; only a new account reads as linked

- On a Portal-managed agent (connector /manage/access/list answers
  managedBy: portal) a code minted with the gateway bearer carries no owner, so
  its redeem binds no account (gateway-gateway scopeService: a managed agent
  takes only the Portal setting). The gateway now refuses to mint there
  (managed_by_portal) and the Channels card on both surfaces lists the linked
  accounts read-only with a 'Link accounts in Nous Portal' button.
- Success is a new platformUserId in the refreshed list, never an 'inactive'
  code before expiry: 'inactive' also means used by an already-linked account
  or unknown. That case now shows a neutral note; expiry still says expired.
- Desktop keys the link card by connection + profile + platform, so a code,
  poll or account list never carries over to another backend.

* docs(telegram): Portal-managed Cloud agents link accounts from the Portal

* fix(relay-link): re-review folds: list survives a failed managed check, fresh snapshot before minting, Portal URL from the login

- The account list no longer disappears when only the access-list question
  fails (managed_by 'unknown'); minting still re-asks and refuses.
- Linking snapshots the linked accounts right before minting, so a failed
  earlier load cannot make every existing account read as newly linked.
- The Portal button follows the instance's Portal (operator override, stored
  login, default) through the scoped resolver instead of raw os.environ.
- Managed copy no longer asks the user to link here.
- Tests: the stand-in connector checks the bearer on the access route and
  answers 404 like a connector without it; the managed test also rejects the
  'Link another' button.

* fix(bot-mode): relaunch no longer pins "Hermes gateway is not connected" in the Bots pane

A relaunch restores the Bots pane before the gateway socket opens. The
gateway Group Chat list fetched groups.capabilities at mount, the request
was refused ("Hermes gateway is not connected"), and nothing re-listed when
the socket opened, so the refusal stayed as a role=alert line under
"Refresh gateway groups" until the user clicked it. Seen with one or two
connections.

Re-list on every gateway state change and only surface a list failure
from an open socket.

* fix(desktop): New Group Chat seats bots from every connected machine again

Since the gateway-owned room driver landed, New Group Chat on any current
gateway offered only the active connection's bots (#141178 was closed by
hiding the others), so a group with one bot on this device and one on
another gateway could not be made at all (#142248).

The picker now offers every reachable bot from every registered connection,
each with a device badge. A selection on the active connection still
creates the gateway-owned room (unchanged control path). A selection that
spans connections creates a Desktop-driven room on the existing round
engine: each member's turn runs on its own gateway through its own route,
and the dialog says plainly that the room runs while this Desktop is open.
Such a room opens in that view on a gateway-owned install instead of the
"Start gateway group" screen. A selection made only of bots on ONE other
connection has no owner reachable from here, so Create stays disabled with
a note to add a local bot or switch connections.

Gateway-owned rooms with peer members are not reachable today: the room
authority reports room_link disabled (canonical_driver_required), so the
Desktop-driven room is the working path until RoomLink lands in Desktop.

Contract change: canonical-group-capability-gates.test.tsx "canonical
creation offers only members on the current connection" (72166e61969, the
picker-only #141178 fix) now asserts the other connection's bot IS offered;
the contradictory-route row stays hidden.

Tests: cross-gateway-group-creation.test.tsx (4 of 5 red on base; the
single-connection control passes on both).

* refactor(desktop): split the group creation engine decision out of the dialog

One table-free decision (groupCreationEngine: gateway / desktop / elsewhere)
and the Desktop-driven room write move out of CreateGroupChatDialog, keeping
the dialog and its create() under the complexity ratchet. No behaviour change.

* fix(desktop): Desktop-driven room members can start a session on the owning gateway

A Desktop-driven room seating a bot on this device (the gateway that owns
local sessions) never got that member's turn to run: "Could not check
default's group session (not_found) — not starting a new one", then, once
past that, "Canonical gateway does not support explicit session options:
room_plumbing". Members on a `hermes serve` connection worked, so a mixed
room answered from the other machine only.

- group-turns.ts: the owner answers a missing session with a 4001 refusal
  whose reason is `not_found` (gateway/session_controls.py), where a serve
  socket answers 4007. isSessionAbsentError accepts both as "mint a fresh
  member session"; every other refusal still fails closed.
- canonical-protocol.ts: `room_plumbing` is the serve socket's flag; the
  owner's create contract has no such field and keys the same behaviour on
  hidden + follow_profile_config, which still ride. Drop it there instead of
  refusing the whole create.

Tests: group-turns-gateway-owned.test.ts (not_found mints, session_busy
still fails closed; the first is red on base) and a canonical-create-options
case for the member-session frame (red on base). Live: e2e/cross-gateway-group.spec.ts.

* fix(desktop): an addressed room member that already answered may pass later

With "@all" (or "@everyone") every member stays addressed for the whole
drive, so a member that answered and then, in the next round, passed on a
teammate's reply was nudged and flagged "explicitly addressed member passed
twice" (#129443's noncompliance row). A two-machine room showed that
error on the local bot right after both bots had answered.

The address is owed only until the member answers: the nudge and the
noncompliance row now apply while the member has no entry in the thread
since the user's latest message. A member that never answers is still
nudged and flagged exactly as before.

Test: group-rounds.addressed.test.ts "@everyone: a member that answered is
not nudged or flagged…" (red on base); the three #129443 cases still pass.

* test(desktop): live two-gateway proof for a cross-machine group chat

e2e/cross-gateway-group.spec.ts: This device (Electron-managed, multiplexing
gateway with the room driver on) plus a real second `hermes serve` under its
own HOME as "Homelab", one mock inference server. New Group Chat must offer
Homelab's bot with a device badge, create the mixed room with the
"Runs while this Desktop is open" note, and an @all send scripted per member
must land both replies in the room log, render, and each reply must exist in
its own gateway's state.db only; the room settles with no member error. A
control test keeps a this-device-only selection on the gateway-owned room.
CROSS_GATEWAY_LEG=main records the picker (repro: Homelab's bot absent).

* fix(desktop): a follow-up sent during an addressed member's turn stays owed

Greptile: "Earlier answers hide unanswered follow-ups". A second @builder
question in the same thread is queued without an epoch bump, so the first
answer can land after it; the positional "answered since the last user
entry" scan then counted that late answer for the follow-up, and a pass on
the follow-up got neither the nudge nor the noncompliance notice.

A turn whose delta carries a user entry it never saw now always owes the
addressed answer; the positional scan only decides turns that saw no new
user entry (a pass on a teammate's reply after answering).

* fix(desktop): a cross-machine room stays Desktop-driven after a membership edit

Greptile: "Membership edits hide the room". Who drives a non-canonical room
was re-derived from its live members on every render, so removing the last
remote bot from a 2-local + 1-remote room sent it to GroupExecutionGate
("Start gateway group", history hidden; starting forked a new room).

The room record now carries `engine: 'desktop'`: stamped at creation when the
selection spans connections, and by either membership door when the room
spans machines before or after the edit (so unstamped older rooms are pinned
at their first edit). Storage hydration and the ui_meta projection keep the
mark (sticky union), so a reload or a second Desktop does not drop it. The
membership check remains only for records without the field.

* fix(desktop): removing a remote member in Manage members takes effect

Independent review P1 (remote member removal does not take). The room-side
picker rewrote groups[] only for local bots, while member resolution seats
any roster row, remote ones included, whose groups[] names the room. The
removed Homelab bot was re-seated by its own metadata, took the next @all
turn and the header kept saying 3 bots. Write the membership patch for
every added or removed row; saveBotMeta routes a remote row to its owner,
as the per-Bot Manage groups door already does.

* fix(desktop): a Desktop-driven room's engine reaches plugin storage

Independent review P2 (engine: 'desktop' is never written to local
storage). updateGroupChat's durable map, durableGroupChatRooms (the
post-merge rewrite) and disband's rewrite of the remaining rooms all
rebuilt each room field by field without `engine`, so hydration read a
field no writer stored and an edited all-local room fell back to the
membership check whenever the ui_meta mirror had not landed. Spread
stickyGroupEngine(room) in all three. The hydration test now seeds
storage through the real writer instead of by hand, and a new test pins
each writer.

* fix(desktop): Manage members offers only bots New Group Chat would seat

Independent review P3 (Manage members offers route-disagreeing rows). The
room-side picker listed every non-ghost roster row, so a row whose route
names another connection, which New Group Chat hides on purpose, could be
seated in a Desktop-driven room through this door. Add candidates now come
from seatableGroupBots; current members stay listed.

* fix(desktop): New Group Chat says why when every bot is on another connection

Independent review P3 (New Group Chat enabled when every seatable bot is on
one other connection). Every selection then only drew the "these bots all
live on <connection>" note and a dead Create button. The New… menu item is
now disabled in that case and carries the same note.

* fix(desktop): the canonical adapter keeps room_plumbing's meaning for every caller

Independent review P3 (room_plumbing dropped in the generic canonical
adapter). prepareCreate destructured the flag away, so any future caller's
room_plumbing would vanish without notice. The plugin cannot tell a
canonical route from a serve socket at the call site (that is the
client's native_dial, not exposed to plugins), so the adapter now
translates the flag into what the owner keys the same behaviour on:
hidden plus follow_profile_config. The member-session create this PR adds
sends the same frame as before; a caller sending the flag alone now gets a
hidden, profile-following row instead of a visible one.

* docs(desktop): isSessionAbsentError also covers a row the caller may not read

Independent review P3: the gateway answers not_found for a session the
actor may not read (session_local_title.py folds permission_denied into
it). Say so on the predicate, and why treating it as absent is safe.

* fix(bot-mode): a former remote member's room lines keep their own machine's name

A cross-machine room's transcript named each line's speaker from the seated
members only. Once the Homelab bot was removed from the room (or before its
source painted after a relaunch), its lines fell back to a bare
{ name: 'default' } row and read as this device's "Hermes", while the
Homelab backend reports no title (its roster row reads "Homelab").

groupTranscriptSpeaker resolves the speaker owner-qualified: seated member,
else the live roster row from the line's own source, else a row carrying
that source, so a remote `default` never borrows the local identity.

* fix(bot-mode): removing a member whose machine is offline fails instead of coming back

Delta re-review P2: unchecking a remote member whose machine is unreachable
saved without error, landed only locally, and that machine's unchanged
ui_meta re-seated the member on reconnect.

- Manage members: a seated member on another machine that is offline (or a
  ghost row) cannot be unchecked; the row says "<machine> is offline -
  reconnect to remove <bot>" (every Bot Mode locale).
- setGroupChatMembers refuses before writing when a removed member is shown
  offline, writes other machines' rows first, and when one of them did not
  take the change ('failed' / 'unreachable') puts back every row it already
  wrote and fails before the room record changes, so membership stays the
  same on every machine.
- saveBotMeta still never throws: a routed row whose machine never answered
  the read now reports 'unreachable' instead of the older-gateway
  'unsupported' fallback; other callers keep treating it as non-fatal.

* fix(desktop): room_plumbing maps to follow_profile_config only, as on serve

Delta re-review P3.1: serve's room_plumbing only skips stored runtime
overrides and does not imply hidden, so the canonical adapter leaves
`hidden` to the caller instead of forcing it.

* fix(bot-mode): the legacy room view subscribes to the roster its speaker labels read

Delta re-review P3.2: LegacyGroupChatWorkspace read $lastRoster.get() in
render without a subscription.

* fix(bot-mode): Manage groups refuses a change its offline machine would undo

Same class as 4fb8d21 (Manage members), on the per-Bot "Manage groups"
door: toggling a room for a bot on another machine that is offline (or
does not answer) reported success, landed only in this Desktop's copy,
and that machine's unchanged ui_meta re-seated or dropped the bot again
on reconnect.

- setGroupMembership refuses before writing when the bot's machine is
  shown offline, and writes through writeMembershipChanges, so a remote
  write that does not land ('failed' / 'unreachable') puts the local
  groups[] back and fails before the room's stored seats change.
- Manage groups: for such a bot every room toggle, Create & join and
  Remove from all groups are disabled, with "<machine> is offline -
  reconnect to change <bot>'s groups" (every Bot Mode locale). The
  success toast now waits for the save and a failure shows its error.

* fix(bot-mode): membership edits report only what each machine kept

Final review of #148418 (three P2s):

- A routed save whose read answered but whose profiles.configure then
  dropped or refused is 'failed', not 'unsupported'; only JSON-RPC
  method-not-found (-32601) is the older-gateway fallback. The pooled local
  backend keeps main's documented fallback. A removal can no longer
  "succeed" while the machine still names the room and re-seats the bot.
- Membership edits of one bot (connection + profile) run one at a time
  through a per-bot queue, and the compensation is an inverse delta (drop
  only the room this edit added / re-add only the room it removed) applied
  to the groups[] the bot's own backend holds at write time (saveBotMeta's
  new rebase hook, re-run on every CAS retry). An older failed Join A no
  longer erases a newer acknowledged Join B.
- Every undo's outcome is checked. When one does not land, the save fails
  with a distinct message naming the bot and the machine that may still
  hold the change (memberRollbackFailed, all 9 locales), and this window and
  the room record are re-read from that machine (or keep the change when it
  still does not answer) instead of claiming "Nothing was changed".

* feat(groups): groups.state says how long a member has worked, which tool it is in, and the round limit

A gateway-owned room only knew WHO was working. member_activity now also
carries started_at (durable, from the driver task row) and, from the
process running the turn, the tool the member is in right now plus the
time of its last frame (progress_at), so the room can show
"builder · terminal · 1m20s" and tell a bot busy in a tool from one gone
quiet. driver_status also reports max_rounds so the room can say
"Round 2 of 3".

The progress map is in-process and bounded (512 tasks, LRU); a process
that did not run the turn simply omits tool/progress_at.

