* [daveey] worker-placement: a not_claimable reserve lost to the reserved worker's claim is a pickup, not a refusal

Probe t_01a19fd990770095 (hub, 11-03): the Board reserved the card for worker-1, worker-1 claimed it at
05:21:51Z, and the hub's next reserve from a copy without that lease got 409 not_claimable at 05:21:52Z.
worker-placement recorded board_refused and commented "Board placement request refused" although the card
ran fine on worker-1 (the local `claimed` event arrived after the refusal was written).

On not_claimable, when the latest queue observation is an assignment to machine X, pull the feed once and
treat a lease (or a Board `claimed` event newer than that observation) held by X as the pickup: no
board_refused reason, no refusal comment. No current reservation, a failed pull (any error; the
batch goes on), or a lease held by another machine keep the refusal, fenced on the row the Board refused (pre-pull)
so the queue guard does not silence it; every card in the tick is judged against the row and reservation its reserve
was made from (snapshotted before the loop). The extra pull runs at most once per tick. Also rewords the `mixed` code ("Candidates excluded for multiple reasons"), which was
untrue for one candidate carrying several reasons.

Card: t_01a19fdd27fcdcca

* feat(bots): opt-in lead routing for Group Chats

A room's settings gain `routing: everyone | lead` (+ `lead_member_id`), stored on the same
`room.context_set` event as purpose/notes and read as of the message that opened the
discussion. `everyone` is the default and plans exactly as before.

In a lead room, a human message that opens a new thread and names nobody first runs one
routing turn on the lead's member session. The lead answers `{"assign": [handles]}`; the
gateway validates the handles against the roster and appends a gateway-authored
`room.assignment` event, and only those members take the normal round (with a line in their
prompt that the lead gave them the message). An answer naming no member, @all, or a failed
routing turn wakes the lead alone; a routing answer can never broadcast. @mentions,
@all/@everyone, scheduled posts and thread replies keep today's resolution.

The routing turn is an ordinary driver task whose seen_through_seq is the message's seq
minus one (a member turn always sees through its opening message), so it is identifiable
from durable state, its receipt never advances the lead's watermark past the message, and
it is excluded from the round-0 terminal set so a lead that picks itself still answers.
Its answer is published as the assignment, never as a visible reply.

Desktop: canonical-group-lead.tsx holds the Room settings control (Everyone answers /
Lead: <member>), the "Ada gave this to Linus, Grace" row, and markRoomLead() so the
member chips can show a Lead badge; the history mounts the row with one line.

* fix(bots): mark the lead routing turn explicitly, not by its watermark

A member turn whose frozen input or attachment batch stops below its
source message also has seen_through_seq < source_event_seq, so
is_routing_task classified it as a lead routing turn: plan_publication
replaced its real reply with "(pass)" and wrote a bogus room.assignment.
That dropped replies in everyone-mode rooms (the default) whenever a
turn was batch-bounded (test_member_recipients_and_bounded_batches_
preserve_unconsumed_files).

The routing turn now carries payload lead_routing: true, set only by
_lead_round and hashed into its task id. The driver accepts the field
only as exactly true; reconstruct_task_plan rebuilds it and rejects it
unless the turn_id has the routing coordinates (round 0, position 0,
seen = source - 1, no frozen input or files), so neither a stripped nor
a forged flag survives restart. Tasks without the field are ordinary
member turns.

* fix(rooms): a lead's routing answer wrapped in braced prose still assigns

Review finding on #148417 (P3, parse_assignment greedy regex): the DOTALL
`\{.*\}` spanned from the answer's object to the last brace in the lead's
prose (`Pick: {"assign": ["linus"]} (see {notes})`), failed to parse, and
the lead took the message alone. The parser now raw-decodes from each `{`
and uses the first balanced JSON object that carries an `assign` key.

* docs(rooms): a discarded routing turn gives the message to the lead

Review finding on #148417 (P3, cancelled routing turn still wakes the
lead): the behaviour is kept and the assignment_effect docstring, which
said a cancelled routing turn "assigns nothing", now says what happens.
Discard gives up one indeterminate attempt, not the user's message: a
discarded member turn in an everyone room leaves the rest of the round
running, and the routing answer is never a visible reply, so settling the
discussion instead would leave the message unanswered with nothing shown.
Stop already ends the round through the stop fence. A test pins it: no
room.assignment, the lead answers once, the discussion settles.

* fix(rooms): room settings read the current routing under the write lock

Review finding on #148417 (P3, set_room_context reads routing outside the
append transaction): an older client's purpose-only save that read the
routing before a concurrent routing change committed wrote the old
routing back. append_event now accepts a payload function evaluated on
the write-locked connection (after BEGIN IMMEDIATE), and set_room_context
resolves omitted routing fields there. Two-thread test holds the older
save right after its read: red before (routing restored to lead), green
after (the routing change waits for the lock and wins).

* fix(rooms): a retried room settings save returns its first event

Greptile "Notes saves undo routing" (follow-up): the omitted routing
fields were resolved from the current room state even when the event id
already existed, so retrying a notes-only save after another client
enabled a lead re-rendered a different payload and failed as a
conflict. Inside the same write transaction, an existing event for the
id now supplies the omitted fields, so the retry is idempotent; a
different save under that id still conflicts.

* fix(rooms): the lead's routing turn names the attached files

Greptile "Routing overlooks attached files": the routing prompt carried
only the message text, so an attachment-only message gave the lead an
empty line to route on. The prompt now lists each attachment's name,
kind and MIME type; the files themselves still go only to the member
who takes the message.

* fix(bots): a member with id "everyone" can be picked as the room lead

Greptile "Lead choice collides with Everyone": the routing select used
the bare member id as each lead option's value, and `everyone` is a
reserved handle, not a reserved member id, so a member with that id
duplicated the Everyone option and choosing it saved everyone routing.
Member options are now encoded as `member:<id>`.

* fix(rooms): retrying a settings save written before lead routing returns its event

Review finding (P2, hosted_room_context.py): a room.context_set event saved by
#144833 carries only purpose and notes, so retrying it under the same event_id
raised KeyError 'routing'. The retry now resolves omitted fields against that
event with defaults and compares in the event's own schema: a field still at
its default is left out, so the same save is idempotent, while asking for a
lead under that id is still a payload conflict.

* fix(rooms): a rename after the lead routed a message keeps the lead's answer

Review finding (P2, hosted_room_discussion.py _lead_round/plan_next_task): the
planner re-mints the routing task on every replay to find its receipt, and the
routing task id hashed its prompt, which names the room and roster. A rename
between routing and the answer re-minted a different id: the routing receipt
then counted as the lead's own round-0 turn (a lead that assigned itself
settled silent_round) or, after a discarded routing turn, queued a second
routing turn. A routing task's id now hashes its durable coordinates and the
lead_routing flag, never its prompt text; member turns still hash their prompt.

* feat(bots): the room lead wears a Lead badge on its header chip

The redesigned room header (#148422) already renders a Lead badge for a member
flagged `lead`, and canonical-group-lead.tsx already derives that flag from the
room's routing (markRoomLead), but nothing connected them. The room view now
passes the marked roster to the header, so a lead-routed room shows who the lead is.

* test(desktop): pin the clock in the Web Access Start progress tests

The reopened-pane test builds a status that started 125 s ago and expects
"2:05 elapsed". Both that status and the pane's elapsed clock read the real
Date, so a runner pause of a second or more between the two showed
"2:06 elapsed" and failed the test. Fake Date at a fixed time alongside the
intervals; advancing the fake timers still moves the clock.

* fix(webapp): a status read reports the Start it began during

The status route read the Start record after access_status(), whose gateway
control-socket round trip can outlast the Start's last step. A second window
then got the pre-Start config with no Start in flight and showed Stopped with
