* perf(tests): fork each Linux test file from a server that preloaded pytest

A fresh python -m pytest <file> spends about half a second starting the
interpreter and importing pytest and the third-party libraries nearly every
test file loads; over ~7000 files that is most of an hour of CPU per run.
With HERMES_TEST_FORKSERVER=1 (Linux CI) the runner starts one
multiprocessing fork server that imports those once and forks a child per
file. Each child still starts from a clean state: its own session, the
file's environment, python -m pytest's sys.path and argv, combined output and
pytest's exit code. Product code and tests/conftest.py are never preloaded.

* test(desktop): don't wait out the real 5-minute completion grace

'a stalled completion banner is capped' held the wait past the real-clock
