* test(windows): forward PSModuleAnalysisCachePath to the test processes

The hosted Windows images point PowerShell at a shared module analysis cache,
which the job's earlier PowerShell steps (the ARM64 build-deps setup) warm.
run_tests.sh starts the suite from env -i and did not forward the variable, so
every powershell.exe a test spawned fell back to a cold per-user cache and
re-analysed every installed module (Az, AWS, Graph): about 22s per launch on the
arm64 runners. The three slowest Windows files (150-167s each) were almost all
that wait.

* ci(tests): run the Linux suite in three slices

* perf(tests): start the slowest cached test files first

The pool starts files in submission order, which was path order, so a slow
file late in the alphabet (tests/tui_gateway, ~40s each) ran alone at the end
of a slice while the other workers sat idle. Submit by cached duration,
longest first; the cache is already restored on CI for the timeout scaler.

* test(runner): a forked file sees neither the fork server's env nor its cached tempdir

Drives run_tests_fork_child.run from a stand-in server that has its own
variable and has already asked gettempdir(). Without os.environ.clear() or the
tempfile.tempdir reset, the probe file fails.

* fix(runner): say how a green file's pytest process exited

A file can pass every test and still exit non-zero. Its failure dump showed
only the green pytest summary, so the cause (an exit-time hook, a status set in
sessionfinish, a signal) had to be rediscovered by rerunning the whole suite.
Append the child's exit status to the captured output.

* fix(runner): read a forked file's exit status under the fork lock

multiprocessing's Process.start() polls every live child
(multiprocessing.process._cleanup). With the fork server, a child's exit
status is 8 bytes the server writes once on the child's status pipe. When a
worker thread starting the next file polled a child at the moment that child's
own thread called join(), one read got the status and the other got EOF, which
multiprocessing reports as exit status 255. A green file then failed with no
error (CI: tests/agent/test_openrouter_silent_default_purge.py, 5 passed, exit
255). Stress repro: 283 of 3000 children reported 255; 0 with this change.

Wait on the sentinel without reading, then join and read exitcode while holding
the lock that also serialises start().

* ci(setup-pm): keep the cached Git entry valid on Windows

Git for Windows ships etc/mtab as a Cygwin system-file symlink, and PM's digest
covers those 38 bytes. @actions/cache restores the PM store with Git's MSYS tar
under MSYS=winsymlinks:nativestrict, which writes a symlink to a non-Windows
target (/proc/mounts) as a WSL reparse point (tag 0xa000001d). Python cannot
open that, tree_digest raised, PM's verification of the cached Git entry
failed, and every Windows job re-extracted the 56 MiB PortableGit archive.

Before PM verifies the store, recreate each WSL symlink with Git's own ln under
winsymlinks:sys. PM's check still decides whether the entry is intact.

* ci(tests-os): warm PowerShell's module analysis cache before the Windows tests

Forwarding PSModuleAnalysisCachePath only helps when something already warmed
the cache. Whether the ARM64 build-deps step did depended on how long its
PowerShell stayed up: PowerShell writes the analysis from a timer ~10s after it
runs (AnalysisCache.QueueSerialization), so a process that exits right after
its first cmdlet never saves it. On runs where build-deps finished fast the
cache stayed cold and every PowerShell child a test spawned paid ~22s again
(arm64 test step 206-259s instead of ~130-150s).

Warm it explicitly from a cmd step and stay up until the cache file is
rewritten: ~39s when cold, ~1s when warm. Forced-cold check: a child launched
afterwards started in 0.4s and the test steps ran in 137/146s.

* fix(runner): start the fork server on first use; satisfy the lint lanes

main() is over the code-health ratchet's complexity and length budgets, and
starting the fork server there grew both. The first file now starts it under
the fork lock, before it copies os.environ, so the knob still never reaches a
test. Also: a timeout on the sys.path probe (HX006), a utf-8-sig read of the
