* fix(mcp): accept an issuer differing from the advertised server only by trailing slashes

The SDK compares the RFC 8414 issuer as an exact string, but the advertised
identifier and the document keep their own spellings of a host-only server:
str(AnyHttpUrl) carries the root-path slash while the document preserves the
operator's spelling, so Otter.ai's https://otter.ai document was rejected
against the https://otter.ai/ expected value and discovery never completed
(#132233).

Accept the document through the existing metadata shim when the two sides
differ only by trailing slashes — the convention _metadata_issuer, the
refresh-token issuer binding and the device flow already apply. Any other
difference (another origin, port, or path) still fails the SDK's exact-string
check.

* fix(mcp): accept only the root issuer's trailing slash, via the SDK's issuers_match

Otter.ai's protected-resource document advertises https://otter.ai/ while its
RFC 8414 document names https://otter.ai; the SDK applies its own root-slash
equivalence (issuers_match) only on the legacy no-PRM path, so the PRM path
rejected the pair (#132233).

Narrow the contributor's matcher to that SDK rule instead of stripping every
trailing slash: path-scoped variants (https://h/as/ vs https://h/as) and
doubled slashes stay rejected, as the SDK rejects them. Step in only when the
spellings differ, so ordinary servers keep the SDK's own issuer check and no
longer log a false "differs by trailing slashes" line. One log statement for
both accepted shapes.

Tests move into test_mcp_oauth_issuer_origin.py, reusing its stand-in: the
real Otter pair and its mirror complete the flow (red on main), and a
path-scoped slash variant is still rejected (red on the original matcher).

