* fix(desktop): an unnamed remote primary keeps its own wire key

A primary reached through HERMES_DESKTOP_REMOTE_URL has a route of
{ connectionId: null, mode: 'remote' }. primaryWireConnectionId returned
its null id, and the wire store keyed every null id as the local source.
With a real local source connected as a secondary, the two sockets'
gateway.ready frames overwrote each other's wire. After a legacy remote
reconnect, native local chats read `legacy`: /undo went through
slash.exec instead of the owner's rewind, and images took the legacy
attachment path.

The wire store now keys a source without a registry id by its mode: local
stays LOCAL_CONNECTION_ID, and a remote gets UNNAMED_REMOTE_WIRE_KEY,
which no registry id can take. primaryWireSource hands the established
route (id and mode) to the publisher, and serverOwnsComposerQueue looks up
the owner route, or the window's connection, by the same key.

* refactor(desktop): dial readiness moves out of the gateway client

The public-dial readiness added in this PR pushed api/client.ts past
1,000 lines: per-dial state, a timer, the held `open` subscribers and the
cancellable request wait sat beside session routing and REST helpers.

DialReadiness (api/dial-readiness.ts) now owns each dial's settle, its
readiness timeout, the held `open` and a request's deadline/abort wait.
HermesGateway connects it to its transport: the constructor's
gateway.ready and state listeners, connect(), close(), onState() and
request(). remainingRequestBudget and GATEWAY_NOT_CONNECTED_MESSAGE move
with it; client.ts re-exports the message for its existing importers.
Behaviour is unchanged, and the dial tests pass as they are. client.ts is
899 lines.

* fix(gateway): deliver settled replies before releasing turn media

* fix(gateway): collect terminal media one admission at a time per profile

Delivering before collection removed the wait that kept one collection per session: every settled media turn now started its own off-loop history scan. Each scan holds a worker thread and a pooled read connection for its whole duration, so turns settling while a long scan runs stacked further scans, and the settlement writes and readers behind them waited for threads or fell back to the writer connection.

Queue the rows on one tracked collector per authority instead. Replies still go out before any collection, and stopping or retiring the profile still joins the collector.

* fix(gateway): keep a cancelled drain cancelled when its delivery fails

A drain cancelled while its reply was being handed off waits for the tracked delivery task before re-raising. When that task failed, the second wait raised the delivery error in place of the cancellation, so the drain ended as a dead pump instead of a cancelled task. Log the delivery failure and re-raise the cancellation.

* fix(gateway): queue admission ids, not rows, for terminal media collection

The collector only needs the admission id, but it queued whole admission rows. While a slow history scan runs, later settled turns accumulate in that queue, and an API row can carry inline images and the client's full conversation history, so those payloads stayed in memory until the earlier scans finished. Queue the id and pass it to `_release_media`.

Reported-by: greptile-apps[bot]

* test(gateway): wait for the off-loop media collector before asserting reclamation

Terminal media collection now runs after delivery on a tracked collector, so a settled or discarded turn's bytes are gone once that collector finishes, not when the drain or `resolve_unknown` returns. Two tests asserted the deletion straight after those returned and failed in CI. They now wait for the authority's tracked work, as the settlement tests this branch already updated do.

* fix(gateway): resolving a retired conversation's unknown turn no longer crashes

Idle retirement (`session_retirement`) can let a conversation go between an unknown turn's resolution commit and its loop-side follow-up. `_resolved_unknown` looked the session up unconditionally to see whether the drain still owned the claim, so a discard without a captured answer raised `KeyError` before `_publish_pending` could restore the conversation, skipped cleanup, and left it retired. A retired conversation has no drain: treat it as not drain-owned and let `_publish_pending` restore it, as main does.

* fix(gateway): a resolution the drain owned keeps its media collection

When an unknown turn is resolved while the drain still holds its claim, `_resolved_unknown` leaves publication, delivery and collection to the drain. The drain queued collection only for a turn it settled itself, so if its recovery then failed (a storage error reading the result back, or ownership moving), the resolved turn's media was never queued and stayed on disk until the age-based sweep. The resolution now hands the collection to the drain explicitly, and the drain honours it on every exit.

* fix(gateway): restore a retired conversation before publishing a resolved answer

The previous fix let a discarded unknown turn resolve after idle retirement had let its conversation go, but the captured-answer branch still indexed the session before `_publish_pending` restored it. When retirement won the race between the resolution commit and its loop-side follow-up, resolving with a captured answer raised `KeyError`, skipped the completion publication and the receipt. This also fails on main before this branch. `_resolved_unknown` now brings a retired conversation back before deciding who owns the claim, so both the captured-answer and discard cases publish and return their receipt; a drain-owned resolution is unchanged.

