* fix(desktop): match a repeated tool event only on the same call

Three gaps in telling a copy of a finished call from a different call:

- A start matched when its arguments were a subset of the finished call's,
  so a constant-id call that dropped an optional argument lost its live
  row. Compare the exact key set instead, leaving out the keys the event
  itself folds into a row (`context`, `preview`, labels), and drop the
  non-empty guard so an argless call's repeated start also matches.
- The scan stopped at a projected queued prompt, which belongs to the next
  turn, so a copy arriving while one was shown drew a second row.
- An older turn's sealed, result-less row with the same id won over the
  current call, so a copy of the current call's completion landed on the
  old row. The waiting row now wins only when it is newer than the
  finished match.

The doc comment now names the case content cannot separate: an older
constant-id backend running the same command twice in one turn. Current
backends rename a reused id before tool.start goes out.

Co-authored-by: BearHuddleston <196404565+BearHuddleston@users.noreply.github.com>

* fix(desktop): tell a late tool copy from a new call by its arguments and turn

Three cases the previous two commits got wrong:

- With no turn live, every running event took the late-completion route.
  A new call whose message.start was missed (an optimistic send across a
  connection gap) also arrives with no turn live, and could take over an
  older turn's sealed row with the same id. That route now needs the
  start to carry exactly that row's arguments; otherwise the reuse rule
  applies and the new call gets its own row.
- A projected queued prompt was always passed over. Once its turn has
  started, before history replaces the projected row, it is that turn's
  boundary. It is now passed over only while the current turn still has
  a response, the rule completeAssistantMessage uses.
- A todo tool's completion carries a `todos` snapshot that is folded into
  the row's args, so a repeated start no longer matched exactly. `todos`
  is now one of the event-added keys, ignored unless the start carries it.

The argument comparison moves into sameCallArgs, shared by both checks,
and the routing into toolEventOwnerMessageId.

* fix(desktop): keep a started queued prompt as its own turn's boundary

The repeat scan passed over a projected queued prompt whenever the session
had a current response (`streamId`, `sawAssistantPayload` or a pending
interim). Once the queued prompt's own turn started and its first
message.delta arrived, those flags were set again. The scan then walked past
that turn's prompt into the previous turn, and a new call reusing the
previous turn's id and arguments was dropped as a repeat, along with its
completion.

Neither those flags nor where rows sit can tell the two turns apart: after
an interim seal, the running turn's next reply is also drawn below the
queued row. What does tell them apart is message.start. Resume projects the
queued prompt while the running turn is already under way, so the next
message.start after the row is drawn is the queued prompt's own turn.
handleMessageStart now records that on the row (`queuedTurnStarted`), and
queuedPromptAwaitsNextTurn passes over a queued row only until then. The
mark lives on the row, not in session state, because every turn's queued
row shares one id; a later projection for a new queued prompt starts
unmarked.

* fix(desktop): keep an earlier turn's interim-sealed tool row out of a new turn

A running tool event re-arms a sealed row without a result only when that
row's message is still in flight: `pending`, or `interim`. But `interim` only
records that a `message.interim` sealed the bubble. It stays set after the
turn ends: when the tool's completion is lost and the final reply streams
into its own bubble, `completeAssistantMessage` settles that bubble and leaves
the sealed one marked `interim`. A later turn that reuses the tool id then
re-armed the old row and drew its command over the earlier one, with no row
of its own.

`handleMessageStart` now records the newest row on screen as the turn's
anchor (`turnAnchorId`). An `interim` row at or above the anchor belongs to an
earlier turn, so a running event no longer re-arms it. Inside the turn the
rule is unchanged: a running copy of a call whose bubble an interim sealed
still updates that row. If the anchor row is no longer on screen, the old
rule applies.

Tests (late-tool-events.test.tsx):
- turn A seals its call with an interim, loses the completion and settles a
  separate final; turn B reuses the id. B gets its own row and A's row keeps
  its command (fails without the anchor check: 1 row instead of 2).
- a running copy of the call after an interim seal in the same turn still
  updates the one row.

* fix(desktop): publish a queued prompt's started mark to the view

`queuedTurnStarted` is listed in COMPARED_FIELDS, but
`chatMessagesEquivalent` never compared it. When `message.start` changed
only that mark, the view-sync flush treated the transcript as unchanged and
skipped `setMessages`, so `$messages` kept the unmarked queued row. Resume
reads `$messages`, so it could seed session state with the old row, and the
repeat check would then pass over a prompt whose turn had already started.

Compare it with `interim` through one table of optional flags, which also
keeps `chatMessagesEquivalent` under its complexity cap.

